# Delivery workflow and communication formats

## Scope
Use this workflow for the Three.js application, including the initial working scene. Asset inventory and repository preparation can precede product planning. Preserve the first-session setup boundary in `START_HERE.md`: preparation does not authorize building the full product.

## Required skills
The locally inspected Matt Pocock skill collection describes this route:

1. `/setup-matt-pocock-skills`: configure the tracker and domain-document layout before the first engineering flow.
2. `/grill-with-docs`: clarify the experience and retain decisions in domain documentation.
3. `/to-spec`: synthesize the agreed plan/spec and identify observable interfaces to test.
4. `/to-tickets`: create self-contained, demonstrable tickets with acceptance criteria and blocking dependencies.
5. `/implement`: implement a ticket using `/tdd` at agreed interfaces, run checks, use `/code-review`, and commit.

Read the actual installed skills and their referenced materials before using them. Verify that they are available to Claude Code in this project; presence in another agent's skill directory does not establish that. If missing, resolve the source and install using the appropriate supported procedure, or report the missing capability. Do not silently replace these named skills with generic instructions and claim the skills ran.

This starter does not bundle or install the skills. The push/PR/merge rules and communication templates below are project conventions layered onto the inspected skill flow; they are not claimed to be Matt's own formats.

## One-time preparation
Run the setup skill against the actual destination repository. Reuse an existing tracker. When none exists, recommend a local markdown tracker until a remote is selected. Follow the setup skill's choice and confirmation steps; keep resulting tracker configuration under `docs/agents/` and link it from `CLAUDE.md`.

Establish repository destination, base branch, authentication, and branch protections before remote operations. If unavailable, complete local preparation and report exactly what remains. Never invent a remote repository or claim a push or merge happened locally.

## Plan and ticket cycle
1. Clarify the product brief with `/grill-with-docs` and write the spec with `/to-spec`. Agree on which externally observable behaviors the tests will cover before writing tests. Explain these choices in plain language.
2. Use `/to-tickets` to propose independently verifiable slices and real blocking dependencies. Obtain the ticket-breakdown agreement required by that skill, then publish tickets to the configured tracker. Establish which tickets are authorized for implementation.
3. Create a dedicated branch for each authorized ticket from the current integration branch. Work only on tickets whose blockers are complete. Prefer a fresh session for each ticket; rely on its spec, criteria, and recorded decisions rather than unrecorded chat history.
4. Use `/implement` with `/tdd`: one failing behavior test, verify it fails for the intended reason, implement enough to pass, then repeat. Test public behavior rather than private renderer internals. Keep refactoring in the review stage as directed by the installed TDD skill.
5. Run the relevant checks and `/code-review`. Address actionable findings and rerun affected checks. Inspect representative 3D views in the browser as well: automated tests alone do not establish correct appearance. Document any part that cannot be tested or inspected.
6. Commit only the ticket's intended changes. Push the branch and open a PR when those actions are authorized for this repository. Include evidence and asset effects using the format below.
7. Merge only when the ticket's acceptance criteria and required checks pass, required reviews are satisfied, the branch is current enough for repository policy, and merge authorization is established. Resolve conflicts by intent and rerun affected checks. Respect repository protections.
8. Verify the remote merge result before marking the ticket done. Record the PR and merge commit, and start the next unblocked authorized ticket. A local commit or open PR is not a completed merge cycle.

If no merge convention exists, propose squash merging: one descriptive integration commit per ticket. Reuse an established repository convention instead. The agent may perform the complete cycle without repeated permission questions when the owner has already authorized it; missing authorization or a required decision must be resolved at the relevant boundary.

## Ticket contents
Use the configured skill/tracker template. Every ticket must identify its parent spec, intended user behavior, acceptance criteria, blockers, and relevant agreed test interfaces. Record test evidence, PR reference, and final outcome on completion. Keep tracker status names consistent with its actual configuration.

## Progress message format
Use short plain-language updates when the stage changes, a meaningful result arrives, or a decision is needed. Avoid narrating every command.

Stage: [Planning / Ticketing / Implementing / Reviewing / PR / Merging]
Ticket: [ID and short title, or not assigned]
Result: [What changed or what was learned]
Next: [Next concrete action]
Decision needed: [Include only when an answer is required, with a recommendation]

Example:
Stage: Reviewing
Ticket: 02 — Reset the object view
Result: Reset restores the camera position; the behavior test passes.
Next: Check the result with the supplied model in the browser.

## Commit message format
Use the repository's established convention. Otherwise use:

<type>(<scope>): <concrete change>

[Optional explanation of why the change is needed.]

Refs: <ticket ID or path>

Types: feat, fix, refactor, test, docs, chore, build, ci, perf.
Use an imperative subject. Include a scope only when useful. Never claim tests passed in the message unless they did.

Example:
feat(viewer): restore initial camera framing on reset

Refs: 02-reset-view

## PR title and description format
Title: <type>(<scope>): <observable outcome>

Description:

### Outcome
[Problem and resulting behavior. Link the ticket and parent spec.]

### Changes
[Short list of changes needed to understand and review the outcome.]

### Verification
- Automated: [Actual checks run and their results.]
- Browser/3D: [Actual asset, interaction, and visual checks performed.]
- Unverified: [Material gaps, or “None” only when accurate.]

### Asset changes
[Added/changed delivery assets, size effects, dependencies, and provenance where relevant. Write “None” when no assets changed. Keep raw archives out of the PR unless intentionally tracked.]

### Review notes
[Material limitations, risks, deployment/configuration steps, or “None”.]

Prefer links to existing previews and attach them when supported. Keep descriptions proportional to the change. Use automatic issue-closing keywords only where supported by the tracker and consistent with the intended completion policy.

## Merge format
Follow the repository's allowed merge method. Default proposal when no convention exists: squash.

Squash commit title:
<PR title> (#<PR number>)

Squash commit body:
[One short paragraph describing the delivered behavior and reason.]

Verification: [Brief actual results.]
Refs: <ticket reference>

For a required merge commit, retain the platform/repository merge-message convention and include the PR reference. For rebase merging, retain the reviewed commit messages. Avoid creating a redundant custom merge commit when squash or rebase is used.

## Completion message format
Ticket: [ID — title]
Delivered: [Observable outcome]
Verified: [Checks actually performed]
Commit: [Commit reference]
PR: [Link, or explicitly not created]
Merge: [Merged commit and base branch, or explicitly not merged]
Remaininx
